#{[17592186362539 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={∬}_{D}\\underbrace{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }\\mathbf{F}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}\\underbrace{(3x{y}^{2})}_{P}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+\\underbrace{(-{y}^{3})}_{Q}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$"] [17592186365398 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}z}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}z}.$"] [17592186365405 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}3{y}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186365412 "Recall that the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=49$ for $y\\ge 7$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =49{sec}^{2}⁡t-49{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =49({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,t) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}7\\operatorname{sec}t},\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{tan}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}7\\operatorname{sec}t}.$"] [17592186365419 "Recall that the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$ for $x\\ge 1$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-\\frac{(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-\\frac{16{tan}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}\\operatorname{sec}t},y,\\hspace{0.167em}4\\operatorname{tan}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}\\operatorname{sec}t}.$"] [17592186365426 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"] [17592186365433 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}4\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"] [17592186365440 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+25{z}^{2}=100\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}5\\operatorname{cos}t}.$"] [17592186369291 "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $1,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Γ}={∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(r(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-16\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.278em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373118 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}(\\operatorname{cos}u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ {e}^{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(u(t)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(u(t)) \\\\ {e}^{u(t)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(4t-3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(4t-3) \\\\ {e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186378566 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-4\\cdot k-2\\cdot 5)-4(8\\cdot k-0\\cdot 5)+6(8\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-32k-80-32k+96 \\\\  & =16-64k \\\\  & =16(1-4k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 16(1-4k) & =0 \\\\ 1-4k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186378573 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =6+1-12 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-5|=5.\n$$"] [17592186378746 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\ny+{x}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ge -{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y\\ge -{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve $y=-{x}^{2}$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b7a6dbbab890b469cf389fa27627e9c1a5f95d8892507fcd4ba5f70541936ea.png)"] [17592186378773 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x+y\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"] [17592186378780 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx+2>0\\,⟹\\,x>-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x>-2\\}.\n$$"] [17592186378787 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y+2\\ge 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-y\\ne 0\\end{cases}\\,⟹\\,\\begin{cases}y\\ge -2 \\\\ y\\ne 2{x}^{3}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -2,\\,y\\ne 2{x}^{3}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186378808 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arcsine has values between $-1$ and $1.$\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1\\}.\n$$"] [17592186378815 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression under the square root must be non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le1 \\\\ y\\ge 0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x+\\sqrt{y}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$"] [17592186378829 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+y\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ne -{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y\\ne -{x}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ and shade *above* and *below* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c85783dc6626a3fea9d06431ee31e202da8c4d88e2eb1c2b6608dccdc2b782e.png)"] [17592186379355 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{k}^{2} & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-1))+2(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0)+5(1\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(6+4)+2(2)+5(-1) \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}+4-5 \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}$ or $k=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}.$"] [17592186383314 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & ={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)(y-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(y-1) \\\\ z & =2y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2y-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-2$ (or $2y-z-2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"] [17592186385494 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & ={f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)(y-0) \\\\ z-2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ (or $y+2z-4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-2,1⟩.$"] [17592186386390 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e4/e48034aed1f41cf1f9b30c057186879233fe05cd2ba720dcee436398a732b6ce.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386397 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386480 "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-v\\operatorname{sin}u⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{sin}v & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}v & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}v & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}v & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v+uv\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{cos}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387554 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}{(\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}+2\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387561 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ((\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}t(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)+t\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =t({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387754 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=4,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387859 "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2(t-1)⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.167em}2t,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-2⟩+⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2t-2,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387866 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{t-1}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(1)-2\\mathbf{g}(1))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(1)-2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(1) \\\\  & =⟨2(1),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{1-1}⟩-2⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3(1)^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩-2⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩-⟨2,6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387880 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})=-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -{t}^{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}-{t}^{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t}(3-t) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387901 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\sqrt{u})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})=-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t))\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u(t)}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{-t}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2{e}^{-2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387908 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}(u+1)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(u(t))^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{u(t)+1},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{t}{1+t},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387971 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3} & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}⟩, \\\\ \\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ t & 5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}t & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 5 \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}⟩+⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\hspace{0.167em}2{t}^{5}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{3}-10t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\hspace{0.167em}6{t}^{5}-2t,\\hspace{0.167em}4{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388932 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388981 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-x\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,x\\ne 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x\\ne 2\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $x=2$ and shade *right* and *left* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/23/2371c9724d66e0f7d74c45e173f9625a986615185c10d55c029976baa82d0877.png)"] [17592186388988 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-2x>0\\,⟹\\,y>2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y>2x\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $>$ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed line $y=2x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/42/42e2faf3df63b038b57d2fddc9b7ef2c2e720dae6e0a8524d602b7ac93681b3d.png)"] [17592186388995 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}(x.y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the point $(0,0)$ is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we exclude the point $(x,y)=(0,0)$ and shade the plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/70/701c54bff1c431883f21cb980ac43f4f2f6beabb11817066a4e909d5a2663a25.png)"] [17592186389511 "Recall that the hyperbola\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=18\\,⇒\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\ge 3$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{sec}^{2}⁡t}{9}-\\frac{2{tan}^{2}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sec}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.167em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}t}.$"] [17592186389518 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}12{x}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}12{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186389525 "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}8{z}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}8{z}^{2}}.$"] [17592186389532 "Recall that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $z\\ge 2$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}-(2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡t-4{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4({sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{tan}t},\\hspace{0.167em}y,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\operatorname{sec}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{tan}t}.$"] [17592186389539 "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{36} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}6\\operatorname{sin}t}.$"] [17592186389553 "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{cos}^{2}⁡t}{25}+\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡t}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{sin}t}.$"] [17592186390737 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}6t,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 12t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}12t & 0 \\\\ 3 & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}12t & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}12t-48{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}-6⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & 2t & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6t & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 6 \\\\ 6t & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}6{t}^{2} & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}6{t}^{2} & 2t \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-28t,\\hspace{0.222em}-24{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & =⟨8t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}12t-48{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}-6⟩+⟨-28t,\\hspace{0.222em}-24{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}36{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20t-2,\\hspace{0.222em}-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\hspace{0.222em}72{t}^{3}-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390798 "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\times \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\times \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-6t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\times \\mathbf{f}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -6t \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & -1 \\\\ -2t & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\times \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t) & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩+⟨0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2-2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186393001 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}2t \\\\ {e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393008 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{cos}u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 3{u}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}(u(t)) \\\\ 3(u(t))^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{u(t)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t}) \\\\ 3({e}^{t})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t}) \\\\ 3{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t}){e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393105 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{5t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 5t{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(3t+1){e}^{3t} \\\\ (4t+1){e}^{4t} \\\\ (5t+1){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393112 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-7)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-7){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393618 "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+1,$ $y={t}^{2}-1,$ and $z={t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =({t}^{2}+1)+({t}^{2}-1)-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393625 "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186397957 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+4u+5v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}3\\sqrt{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186397964 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(3{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}6u,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(3{u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 6u & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}6u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6u \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-6u,\\hspace{0.222em}2,\\hspace{0.222em}-6u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6u)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(-6u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{u}^{2}+4+36{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72{u}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(18{u}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{18{u}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186397971 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}v⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & v \\\\ 2v & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-2{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(-2{u}^{2})^{2}+(4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{u}^{4}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{blue}2\\sqrt{{u}^{4}+{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186400338 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{k}^{2} & 7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(7\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)-7(6\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 4)+4(6\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}+0-84 \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}-84 \\\\  & =14({k}^{2}-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 14({k}^{2}-6) & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =6 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\sqrt{6}$ or $k=\\sqrt{6}.$"] [17592186401226 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186401233 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(-2y)}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186504931 "- Statement $b =-e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=-e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have opposite directions. So, $b\\neq e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b =-e$ is true."] [17592186563656 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0) & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={f}_{x}(\\frac{1}{2},0)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=0 \\\\ z &=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $x-z+\\frac{1}{2}=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-k)=i+k$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186563730 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"]}
